<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mpondo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as amaMpondo, Pondo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mpondo are a Xhosa-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a> people of the north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-cape">Eastern Cape</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, in the region historically called Pondoland, between the Mthatha and Mtamvuna rivers. Their kingdom kept its independence until it was annexed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> in 1894. In 1960 the Mpondo revolt against apartheid's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">Bantustan</a> policy was crushed by the state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mpondo.jpg" alt="Mpondo" width="317" height="526" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrew Geddes Bain (1797 - 1864) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bain_-_Mpondo_haarmodes.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In Mpondo oral tradition the people descend from King Mpondo, grandson of Sibiside, leader of the Embo or Aba-Mbo nation, who are thought to have migrated south into present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Mpondo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpondomise">Mpondomise</a> are remembered as twins; tradition holds that Mpondo, generally taken as the senior, killed a lion and refused to hand its skin to his brother, and then led his followers away to settle elsewhere. The Mpondo share their lineage with the amaMpondomise, amaXesibe, the Mkhize, the amaBomvu and the amaBomvana. The royal clan, the amaNyawuza, takes its name from King Nyawuza, a later descendant of the founder.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Mpondo tradition remembers the reign of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faku">Faku</a> for the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> invasions during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mfecane">Mfecane</a>. The first, in 1824, ended with the Zulu army short of food and foraging for wild watermelons, so that it became known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/impi">Impi</a> yama Bhece. Before a second invasion in 1828 Faku moved women and children into the Mngazi forests west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mzimvubu-river">Mzimvubu River</a> while his son Prince Ndamase led the army, and a third attempt was turned back at the Mthavuna River. The kingdom also fought neighbours such as the Abathembu, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpondomise">amaMpondomise</a> and the Amabhaca.</p><p>In the 1950s the land rehabilitation programme, the Bantu Authorities system, which gave chiefs a limited, state-sanctioned power in place of consultation at the inkundla, and higher taxes provoked growing resistance. In June 1960 police fired on a meeting at Ngquza Hill, killing 11 people and arresting 23. Chiefs seen as collaborators were attacked, and a boycott of white-owned stores followed. By the end of November 1960 a state of emergency covered Flagstaff, Bizana, Tabankulu, Lusikisiki and Mount Ayliff, and heavy policing and military force put the revolt down; 4,769 people were imprisoned, 2,067 brought to trial, and 30 sentenced to death in 1961.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The great house of the kingdom has its palace, Ndimakude, at Flagstaff, and the right-hand house its Nyandeni Great Place at Libode; Nyandeni long enjoyed autonomy as western Mpondoland, while the Qawukeni house was called eastern Mpondoland. Towns of the kingdom include Lusikisiki, Mbizana, Ntabankulu, Port St. Johns and Ngqeleni. Besides the royal clans, the kingdom holds older Aba-Mbo clans and clans that settled later and pay tribute. The Mpondo perform ukusina and their own imfene, or baboon dance, to maskandi music; in the old calendar the year begins in September, the month of the annual Mpondo Culture and Heritage Festival at Ntabankulu.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mpondo people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Mpondo</b> were a southern Nguni people whose royal genealogy reaches back many generations, settled between the Mtata and Mtamvuna rivers by the 17th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/mpondo-kingdom">Mpondo Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mpondomise share their earliest royal genealogy with the <b>Mpondo</b>, from whom they separated many generations before written records.</blockquote><a href="/state/mpondomise-kingdom">Mpondomise Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>By the 18th century the Thembu paramount was recognised as holding senior rank among the southern Nguni, alongside the Xhosa and <b>Mpondo</b> royal houses.</blockquote><a href="/state/thembu-kingdom">Thembu Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
